<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manchus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Manchu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manchus are a people of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. United by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> and his successors, they founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, which conquered Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1644. The Manchus organised their army in the banner system and maintained a separate identity as the ruling elite of the Qing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manchus.jpg" alt="Manchus" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torii Ryūzō · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manchu_people_(No.3036).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Manchus are a Tungusic East Asian ethnic group native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, the region that takes its name from them. Their name, manju in Manchu, was adopted as the official name of the people by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> in 1635, replacing the earlier name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>'s own record derived it from Mañjuśrī.</p><p>The Manchus are said to descend from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> who founded the Jin dynasty, although historians dispute this. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens">Jianzhou Jurchens</a>, from whom they came, originated in part from the Huligai, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> classed as separate from the Jurchens of the Jin. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> reorganised the Jurchens into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a>, many Manchu clans were artificially created from unrelated people, who took a place name as their clan name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Manchus founded the Later Jin dynasty (1616 to 1636) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> (1636 to 1912). After 1644 the majority of the people living in the inner city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> were Manchu and Mongol bannermen, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> were not allowed to enter it until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a>. Manchu and bannermen were declared legally equivalent in the 17th century, so banner membership was never genetically uniform, and Manchu identity was traditionally patrilineal. Many Manchu bannermen in Beijing supported the Boxers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a> and sustained heavy casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Manchus were sedentary agriculturalists who lived in permanent villages and practised hunting and mounted archery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> said that riding and archery were the most important martial arts of the country, and the results of yearly tests could affect Manchu rank in the nobility. Manchu men traditionally shave the front of the head and wear the hair at the back in a single braid, the queue, which the Qing made compulsory for Han men under pain of execution for treason.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> ordered the scholars G'ag'ai and Erdeni to make a Manchu script from the Mongolian script, and Dahai later added dots and circles to complete it. Fluency among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">bannermen</a> declined from the early 18th century. Manchus were Buddhists with shamanist influences, and after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, though shamanic traditions persisted. Their most important piece of literature is the Tale of the Nisan Shaman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manchu people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin also became a model for the <b>Manchus</b>: Nurhaci, a leader of the Jianzhou Jurchen, named his state the Later Jin in 1616 before his successor renamed it Qing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Reforms after the war created the Military Training Agency and the Five Army Camps, but they could not stop the <b>Manchus</b>, who invaded in 1627 and again in 1636–1637, forcing King Injo to surrender after a siege at Namhansanseong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Manchuria, the homeland of the <b>Manchu</b> people and by the 20th century overwhelmingly Han Chinese, had been contested since Russia annexed Outer Manchuria by the unequal treaties of 1858 and 1860.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Meanwhile the <b>Manchus</b>, united under Nurhaci and Hong Taiji, pressed on the north-east frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Hong Taiji renamed the people “<b>Manchu</b>” in 1635, distancing them from the Jurchen Jin dynasty that had once ruled northern China, and proclaimed the Qing dynasty in 1636, presenting it as a legitimate successor within the Chinese imperial tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…tutelary gods of nature in “communities of the gods”, with large temple gatherings and a pantheon of more ancient gods; folk sects and mother goddesses are strongest in the central plains and the northeast, where Han religion met Tungus and <b>Manchu</b> shamanism and fox gods head the pantheons.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
